## What I did — RULE 4 loop, decisive

**Followed sess71's coded-but-unbuilt experiment** (P-DIROPEN in `xfs_dir_open`), built `63613E90`, deployed pristine, reproduced Face A (`cat node1_after_1: Is a directory`) at iter 2.

**Got decisive proof** by adding `ip=%px` + `fop_dir=%d` to P-EVICT-RESULT and `ip=%px` to P-DIROPEN (build `51A6A9DC`):
- `P-EVICT-RESULT ino=131 ip=ffff8e0a99f7cd80 final_mode=0100644(REG) inew=0 fop_dir=1`
- `P-DIROPEN ino=131 ip=ffff8e0a99f7cd80 mode=REG` — **same pointer**

→ ONE in-core inode with `i_mode=REG` but `i_fop = xfs_dir_file_operations`. `cat` opens → `xfs_dir_open` → read → `generic_read_dir` → **-EISDIR**.

## Root cause (PROVEN) + fix
An in-place dinode reload that flips S_IFMT (DIR→REG on inode-number reuse) updates `i_mode` in `xfs_inode_from_disk` but never re-wires `i_op`/`i_fop`/`a_ops`. `xfs_setup_iops` runs only on the `XFS_INEW` iget path; the reload returns a live cache-hit (`inew=0`), so the vtable stays stale. **This is why 17 sessions of inode-layer fixes never closed it — the residue was the operations vtable, not the inode.**

**Fix** (`xfs/xfs_mxfs_dlm.c` `mxfs_dlm_reload_inode` ~L1420): capture old S_IFMT before `xfs_inode_from_disk`; if it changed, call `xfs_setup_iops(ip)`. Build `8B1A4AD6`, deployed.

**Validated (RULE 4 step 2b)**: repro went from FAIL@iter2 → **8 consecutive PASS**; `P-RELOAD-IOPS-REWIRE` fired (test2×1, test3×2); **zero "Is a directory" errors** all run.

## Remaining — Face B (now sole failure, ~1/9)
Iter 9 hit **concurrent dir-block lost-update** (sess69 Face B): node3 `mv: cannot stat node3_before_4..8` — a contiguous suffix of its own committed entries clobbered by a peer writing a stale cached shared dir block. Next step (in progress, was reading the code at boundary): instrument `i_dlm_dir_gen` bump on the dir-inode **write-path acquire** vs the dir-block modify, per the H-DIR hypothesis.

State.md, sess72 memory, and MEMORY.md index updated. Cluster on `8B1A4AD6`, pristine+mounted. Criteria **PASS=10 FAIL=2** — marker NOT written (work not done).

